"""Additional narration appended to scenes (video number, scene number starting at 1) so each video reaches its target length."""

EXTRA = {
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- video 1
    (1, 2): "Do not worry about memorising every control right now. Think of the window as a map: sound sources at the top, shaping in the middle, movement in the lower area, and performance controls at the bottom.",
    (1, 5): "Notice that the filter tabs are separate. Anything you change while the Filter 1 tab is showing belongs to Filter 1, and the same goes for Filter 2.",
    (1, 7): "You will spend most of your time in two or three of these tabs. Routing for connections, Amp Env for the shape of the note, and one of the modulation slots for movement.",
    (1, 10): "If you ever want to undo a knob you have turned, the Undo button in the top bar steps back through edits, connections and modulation changes, and Redo steps forward again.",
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- video 2
    (2, 1): "This is also the quickest way to get out of a mess. If a patch has become confusing, load Init Blank and start fresh; you can always press Undo or reload a preset if you change your mind.",
    (2, 3): "Each node has a small activity meter along its bottom edge. Once sound is flowing you will see these meters move, which is a quick way to find out where the signal is and where it stops.",
    (2, 4): "Notice that the ports are on the node edges. Outputs are on the right, inputs on the left, and the Out node only has an input, because it is the end of the line. You can send several sources into the same input and they are mixed together.",
    (2, 5): "If a wire stays dark, no signal is passing, and the cause is almost always upstream. Maybe a level is at zero, or the node before it has nothing connected to it.",
    (2, 7): "The quick setups replace whatever routing you had, and they can be undone. They are a good way to start a patch, then adjust the wires by hand.",
    (2, 9): "Take a moment here and play with Pos while holding a note. This is one of the most expressive controls on the synth, and we will spend the whole next video on it.",
    (2, 11): "A good rule of thumb: short attacks and releases feel percussive, long ones feel like pads. Sustain is a level, not a time, so zero means the note fades away while you hold the key.",
    (2, 12): "That one added effect, a little reverb, makes a plain patch feel like a finished sound. Every effect is bypassed at a mix of zero, so you only pay for what you use.",
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- video 3
    (3, 1): "If you have just come from the second video, you will recognise this setup. If not, pause and build it; it takes about ten seconds.",
    (3, 2): "Drag slowly while holding a low note and listen for the smooth changes. If you hear steps, move slower. The position knob in the panel does exactly the same thing as dragging in the display.",
    (3, 3): "Try the previous and next buttons beside the wavetable menu to flick through tables quickly. They only step through the tables that are loaded, including any you import.",
    (3, 4): "A tip for the choir: the vowels are baked into the waveform, so they move with the note you play. They sound most natural in the low to middle register.",
    (3, 5): "Each of these tables is thirty-two frames long. That is plenty to make smooth sweeps, and they are band-limited, so they stay clean even high up the keyboard.",
    (3, 6): "Intervals are easy. Set Osc 2 to plus seven, a fifth, or plus twelve for an octave. For a thicker single sound, use Fine to detune Osc 2 just a few cents away from Osc 1.",
    (3, 7): "As you raise detune, the stack gets wider and the beating gets faster. Small amounts feel like a warm chorus; large amounts feel like an ensemble of many oscillators. Each voice starts at a random phase, so every note sounds slightly different.",
    (3, 8): "A different table in each oscillator is a quick way to get complex tones. A bell table on Osc 2 under a saw on Osc 1, for example, is a good starting point for keys.",
    (3, 9): "The modulator's pitch sets the ratio between the two oscillators. Whole-number ratios sound harmonic and musical; odd ratios sound like bells and clangs.",
    (3, 10): "A little fold makes a saw more complex and vocal; a lot makes it hollow and harsh. It reacts to level, so unison and the choice of table matter. Try modulating it with an envelope for sounds that start rough and mellow out.",
    (3, 11): "Single-cycle files of two thousand forty-eight samples give the cleanest results, but any audio works. Recordings of voices or instruments turn into evolving textures, and Pos becomes a way to scrub through the recording.",
    (3, 12): "When all twenty-two user slots are full, the last one is replaced, so keep an eye on your library. If a file cannot be read, a message tells you.",
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- video 4
    (4, 1): "Saws are full of harmonics, which gives the filter something to remove. Listening to a filter on a sine wave would teach us very little.",
    (4, 2): "While sweeping, listen to how the resonance peak moves up and down through the harmonics. The bigger the peak, the more vocal and nasal the sound becomes. Back off the resonance if the sound gets harsh.",
    (4, 3): "The state variable filter is a little cleaner and more precise than the ladder, which makes it good for drums and for effects where you want a tidy, controlled response.",
    (4, 4): "A comb filter works best on short or noisy sounds, and on sources with a lot of harmonics. Experiment with different cutoff values until the resonance matches the notes you play.",
    (4, 5): "Between the vowels the filter interpolates smoothly, so sweeping the cutoff with an LFO or envelope makes the sound speak. The vowel positions are roughly one hundred and ten hertz for E, six hundred thirty for I, and three and a half kilohertz for O.",
    (4, 6): "Think of Drive as a way to add warmth and bite before the filter. Because the filter comes after, the saturation gets shaped, so a closed filter turns a harsh drive into a pleasant growl.",
    (4, 7): "Filter 2 has its own model, mode, cutoff, resonance, drive and key track, so the two filters can be completely different. Many classic sounds come from a low-pass and a high-pass working together.",
    (4, 8): "Key Track only changes where the cutoff sits relative to middle C. Use negative values if you want low notes to be brighter than high notes, which is unusual but can be fun.",
    (4, 9): "The insert only does something if a wire enters it. If you ever add the insert and hear nothing, check that it is connected on both sides.",
    (4, 10): "Drive controls how hard the signal is pushed, and mix blends the processed sound with the original. Both can be modulated, so try an envelope on Insert Drive for a bright, crunchy attack that smooths out.",
    (4, 11): "Amp Pan is equal-power, so the level stays consistent as you move across the stereo field. Modulating pan with an LFO gives you auto-pan.",
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- video 5
    (5, 1): "Everything in this video happens with this one simple patch, so you can follow along exactly.",
    (5, 2): "Delay waits before the attack begins, and Hold keeps the envelope at its peak for a moment before it decays. Env Level scales the whole envelope, which is useful when the envelope is used as a modulator.",
    (5, 3): "Decay and release curves are exponential, so they sound natural for plucks and percussion. A new attack always starts from the current value, so retriggering never clicks.",
    (5, 4): "The chip at the top left of each modulation tab is the source. The knob you drop it on is the destination. The ring that appears around the knob shows how far the source can move it.",
    (5, 5): "While you play, a white dot on the knob shows the live modulated value. It is a great way to understand what is actually happening to the sound.",
    (5, 6): "A very small amount on the Pitch knob goes a long way, because the Pitch knob covers ninety-six semitones. Zero point zero zero three is only about a third of a semitone.",
    (5, 7): "Level controls how deep the LFO goes. With the level at half, the same amount produces half the movement, so you can use it as a depth control for the whole slot.",
    (5, 8): "If you are working in a DAW the synced LFO follows the project tempo. In the standalone app, where there is no host tempo, it assumes one hundred and twenty beats per minute.",
    (5, 9): "Because each note has its own LFO, chords can sound alive and slightly different on each note, unless the phase is set to start them all together.",
    (5, 10): "That is why macros, the mod wheel and aftertouch are the way to move effects. They are the same for every note, so they are meaningful for the shared effects at the end of the chain.",
    (5, 12): "You can have up to two hundred fifty-six assignments, several sources on one knob, and one source on many knobs, so do not be afraid to layer modulation.",
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- video 6
    (6, 2): "There are sixteen macros in two groups of eight. Group them in your head: the first eight are the ones you will want to show on a controller.",
    (6, 3): "The name is the full parameter name, such as Filter 1 Cutoff or Reverb Mix, so there is never any doubt what a macro does.",
    (6, 4): "Macros are normal parameters, so they can also be automated in your DAW. Draw an automation lane for Macro 1 and the filter will follow it.",
    (6, 5): "Setting up two macros on the same knob, one positive and one negative, is a good way to build crossfade controls.",
    (6, 6): "Notice the macro for Reverb Mix. Voice modulators cannot control effects, but macros can, which is what makes them ideal for performance controls such as a space knob.",
    (6, 7): "Try assigning the same macro to Osc 1 Pos with plus point four and to Filter 1 Cutoff with minus point two. Turning one knob now changes the timbre and darkens the sound at the same time.",
    (6, 8): "Names are limited when generated, but when you type your own you can be as descriptive as you like. A short, clear name is best, since hardware displays have little room.",
    (6, 9): "Whether a host or controller refreshes the label immediately depends on that host. If the name does not update, re-selecting the plug-in in the host's mapping list usually fixes it.",
    (6, 10): "Mod wheel, sustain and the channel messages are reserved and cannot be learned, but every other controller number can.",
    (6, 11): "A macro assignment is part of the sound. If you load a factory synth preset you will often find macros one to four already assigned to Filter 1 Cutoff, Filter 1 Reso, Osc 1 Position and Reverb Mix.",
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- video 7
    (7, 1): "Notice how few controls there are. A Performer gives you a lot of rhythm and melody with only five knobs and sixteen bars.",
    (7, 2): "Sync follows your project tempo. If you turn it off, the Rate knob sets the speed in steps per second, from one tenth of a hertz up to forty hertz.",
    (7, 3): "Smooth is useful for filter movement, because gliding between steps removes the stair-step sound. For pitch it turns the arpeggio into a wobbling slide.",
    (7, 5): "The amount is a fraction of the knob's travel. The Pitch knob covers ninety-six semitones, so plus point two five is twenty-four semitones.",
    (7, 6): "If the highest bar is not quite where you want it, just change the amount, or redraw the bar. Because each note has its own Performer, chords arpeggiate together if you play them at the same moment.",
    (7, 7): "Saving a pattern is also a nice way to build a library of rhythmic gates and melodic lines that you can drop into any patch.",
    (7, 8): "The Performer restarts at note-on, so for tight rhythms play the notes on the grid. The delay in time with the tempo fills the gaps and makes simple patterns sound big.",
    (7, 9): "Why set Amp Level to zero? Because the Performer adds to the knob position. If the knob were at its default, the gate would never fully close.",
    (7, 11): "You can use all three Performers at once, at different step counts, to create evolving polyrhythms. A five-step and an eight-step Performer drift in and out of phase.",
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- video 8
    (8, 1): "Because the oscillator goes through filter one, you can use the filter to tame it, while the noise bypasses it for a clean click.",
    (8, 2): "The insert with a mix of one makes the whole signal go through the saturation, adding harmonics to a kick that would otherwise be a plain sine, which helps it be heard on small speakers.",
    (8, 3): "Sustain at zero is important for drums, because it makes the sound die away by itself. Without OneShot, letting go of the key early would send the envelope into its release and cut the drum short.",
    (8, 4): "The decay of Mod 2 sets how fast the pitch drops. Shorter makes a clicky, tight kick; longer makes a deeper, boomier one. The amount sets how much extra pitch the attack has.",
    (8, 6): "The state variable filter in band-pass mode is a very good snare shaper: lower the cutoff for a boxier snare, raise it for a snappier one.",
    (8, 7): "You can make a shaker the same way, with a short hold and a slightly lower high-pass. And for something more metallic, try the Crackle noise type or a comb filter on the noise.",
    (8, 8): "Setting the Amp Level to zero seems odd, but the Performer will add the level back, step by step, which is exactly what we want for a series of hand claps.",
    (8, 9): "A Smooth of about thirty percent softens the edges of each hand, so the clap does not click. Longer smoothing gives a washy, softer clap.",
    (8, 10): "Toms are sines like kicks but pitched higher, with a shorter sweep and a longer tone. Play a fill up the keyboard and you have a tuned tom set.",
    (8, 11): "Remember the drum presets use a OneShot amp envelope so a tap plays the whole hit. Play around C zero to C three. The preset list is a good place to compare your patch with ours.",
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- video 9
    (9, 1): "Sub bass is the foundation, so we keep it simple: sines and a low-pass filter. Complexity can come later.",
    (9, 2): "Always check a bass on more than one speaker system. A very low sine can be felt but not heard on laptop speakers, which is why harmonics from the insert are so useful.",
    (9, 3): "In mono mode each new note retriggers the envelopes and slides from the last pitch. If you want slides that do not retrigger, use legato mode, which we will use for the acid line.",
    (9, 4): "Unison with a small detune gives the saw some width. For a wobble, a little detune is plenty, since the movement will come from the filter.",
    (9, 5): "The depth of the wobble depends on both the base cutoff and the amount. If the wobble disappears at the lowest point, raise the base cutoff a little or lower the amount.",
    (9, 6): "The envelope here is very short and sustain is zero, so each note has a snap. With a long note the filter closes again, leaving the resonant tail of the oscillator.",
    (9, 7): "Because velocity is a voice source, each note has its own value. Pressing harder opens the filter more on that note.",
    (9, 8): "Two detuned stacks beating against each other is the heart of the reese. The fine control on Osc 2 changes the speed of the beating, so try values from plus five to plus fifteen.",
    (9, 9): "Slow modulation is the secret. At point three hertz the filter takes about three seconds per cycle, so it feels like the bass is breathing.",
    (9, 10): "Add a little fold or tanh after the filter and the reese gets a growl. Because the insert comes after the filter, it distorts the filtered signal, which is the sound you want here.",
    (9, 11): "Try pressing Mutate on any of these patches. It will randomise the continuous voice parameters a little, which is a quick way to discover variations. Undo takes it back.",
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- video 10
    (10, 1): "Eight voices can sound huge, but they also use a lot of processing, especially when you play chords. If your computer struggles, reduce the unison or the polyphony.",
    (10, 2): "A touch of chorus makes the supersaw wider, and the reverb places it in a space. Notice we leave the delay for the lead patch.",
    (10, 3): "Mono mode plays one note at a time. Combined with a very short glide, it gives that smooth connection between notes that makes a lead feel like an instrument.",
    (10, 4): "Everything here is an assignment you can edit. If the mod wheel changes too much, right-click the knob and lower the amount. A lead should feel responsive, not wild.",
    (10, 5): "The fade in is important. A vibrato that is present at the start of a note sounds mechanical, while one that arrives after a moment sounds like a singer or a violinist.",
    (10, 6): "A pad needs time. The long attack means notes swell in, and the long release means they overlap and blend into a continuous wash.",
    (10, 7): "With the brightness and the space under your fingers, you can bring the pad from dark and distant to bright and close, which is often all a pad needs to fit into a track.",
    (10, 8): "The position knob also makes a great target for a macro. Dragging a macro onto Pos lets you move through vowels by hand.",
    (10, 9): "If you want a more natural voice, keep the pitch low, and use fewer unison voices, since more voices blur the vowel.",
    (10, 10): "For a longer swell like a film brass, increase the amp attack to a tenth of a second and give the filter envelope a slower attack and a bigger reverb.",
    (10, 11): "Looking at how the factory presets are built is one of the best ways to learn. Load one, open the tabs, and see which knobs have rings.",
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- video 11
    (11, 1): "The User category shows your own presets, and All shows everything together, factory and user.",
    (11, 2): "Presets load instantly, and the Undo history is cleared when a preset loads, so Undo will not take you back to the previous sound.",
    (11, 3): "If you save with a name that already exists in that category, the file is replaced, so use new names for variations.",
    (11, 4): "A user preset stores every parameter, the routing, all modulation assignments with their amounts, the macro names, the three Performer patterns and any imported wavetables, so it is complete and portable.",
    (11, 5): "Because it only touches continuous voice parameters, mutate will never change your routing, wavetable choice, filter models or effects, so it is safe to use on a patch you like.",
    (11, 6): "If you use the computer keyboard to play, click the keyboard first so it has focus. And the keys lighting up when MIDI arrives is a handy way to check that your controller is connected.",
    (11, 7): "Learn macros for performance knobs, and use learn on individual parameters like cutoff or volume for hands-on control. The assignment stays with your DAW project, so each project can have its own mapping.",
    (11, 8): "A bigger interface makes small knobs and Performer bars easier to hit, and a smaller one saves screen space. The setting is remembered between sessions.",
    (11, 9): "The scale repeats every period, usually an octave, and degree zero sits on middle C. If a file is not a valid scale file, a message says so.",
    (11, 10): "Single-cycle waveforms of two thousand forty-eight samples sound cleanest. Longer audio is split into frames and resampled, up to two hundred fifty-six frames.",
    (11, 11): "Tables are stored inside user presets and inside your DAW project, but factory presets only ever use the built-in tables.",
}
